title: Exec credential tokens are fetched once and never refreshed (expirationTimestamp
  ignored)
updated: 2026-10-18T06:32:30Z
---

File-based tokens rotate through `Kubernetes::REST::AuthTokenFile`, but an exec plugin's result is frozen into a plain `AuthToken` (lib/Kubernetes/REST/Kubeconfig.pm:513-517). `status.expirationTimestamp` is not read (`grep -rn expirationTimestamp lib t` is empty), and `status.clientCertificateData`/`clientKeyData` (plugins that hand out certificates instead of a token) end in 'exec credential did not return token'. EKS tokens live 15 minutes, GKE/AKS about an hour: a long-running process built from such a kubeconfig - a `watch` loop, a Net::Async::Kubernetes watcher or controller, a Comb daemon - starts getting 401 after that and can only recover by rebuilding the client.

Done when: an exec-backed credential object re-runs the plugin once the reported expiry is near (or on demand after a 401), mirroring the AuthTokenFile approach, and the POD of `api` states what is supported; certificate-returning plugins either work or fail with a message that says so.

